// The ZIP archive: an .odt, a .docx and a .jar are all this format.
//
// The whole file is read into an `int[]`, one byte per cell. That is the
// expensive choice and the right one: a ZIP is read from the end and then
// jumped around in, so a streaming reader would need seeks the fs backend
// does not offer, and a document is a few hundred kilobytes.
//
// The directory is parsed into exported parallel lists -- names, methods,
// sizes, offsets -- so one archive is open at a time. zip_find and zip_read
// take the byte buffer as well, because that is the argument that says *which*
// archive the caller means even though only one can be current.

// The bytes of any file, directory or not. inflate needs this too, for a raw
// DEFLATE stream that never went through a ZIP.
zip_bytes(string path) {
  int[] xs = [];
  zip_size(path, xs);
  zip_load(path, xs);
} return int[] xs;

// The archive, ready for zip_find and zip_read.
zip_open(string path) {
  int[] xs = zip_bytes(path);
  zip_dir(xs);
} return int[] xs;

// fs_size returns -1 for a file that is not there, and lst_fill of -1 cells
// is silently an empty buffer -- so the size is checked here rather than
// discovered later as an index trap with nothing naming the file. Nothing is
// said here about how big an *archive* has to be: a raw DEFLATE stream is
// four bytes, and this is also the way one is read.
zip_size(string path, int[] xs) {
  int n = fs_size(path);
  if (n < 0) {
    inf_fail("zip: cannot read " + path);
  }
  lst_fill(xs, n, 0);
} return void;
